#{[17592186093402 "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\t=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA^{-1} =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$"] [17592186116491 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y &={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand let\n$$\nA = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{12}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{red}{a}_{22}}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{x} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{b} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{b}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n2\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n3\\. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n4\\. The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n5\\. The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)."] [17592186116498 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n$$\n[{\\color{blue}{A}}\\, |\\, {\\color{black}I_2}]\t\n=\t\n\\left[{\\begin{array}{cc|cc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}7} & 0 & 1\\end{array}}\\right]\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}7 & - 2 \\\\ - 3 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121018 "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle det \\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}\\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 2 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\color{red}0} & \\displaystyle {\\color{red}2} & \\displaystyle {\\color{red}0} & \\displaystyle {\\color{red}0} \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 3\\end{array} \\right] & \\displaystyle  = \\sum _{j = 1}^{n} {\\color{red}{a}_{3 j}} {\\color{black}{C}_{3 j}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{31}} + {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}{C}_{32}} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{33}} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot  {\\color{black}{C}_{34}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}( - 1 {)}^{3 + 2}} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 3\\end{array}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = - 2 \\cdot  \\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\displaystyle - 1 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle 0 & \\displaystyle - 3\\end{array}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant."] [17592186121019 "We can also use the Laplace expansion across any *column* to find the determinant. Across the column $j,$ the Laplace expansion is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{1j}{C}_{1j}+{a}_{2j}{C}_{2j}+⋯+{a}_{nj}{C}_{nj},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}C_{ij}$ is the cofactor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{ij}}.$\n\nIn other words, the Laplace expansion across a row or column is the sum of the entries in the row or column multiplied by their respective cofactors.\n\nTo find the determinant of a matrix, we can choose to use the Laplace expansion across any row or column, so choose the row or column that has the most zeros, to cut down on the calculations!"] [17592186125785 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$"] [17592186125786 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$."]}
